<article class="env-proposition" id="0F3S">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0F3S">Proposition <span data-tag="0F3S">37.77.2</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a surjective quasi-finite morphism of schemes. Let $w : X \to \mathbf{Z}_{> 0}$ be a positive weighting of $f$. Assume $X$ affine and $Y$ separated<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000864181" id="a0000864181-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> and nonempty. Then the affine stratification number of $Y$ is at most the number of distinct values of $\int _ f w$ minus $1$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Note that since $Y$ is separated, the morphism $X \to Y$ is affine (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01SG" data-tag="01SG">29.11.12</a>). The function $\int _ f w$ attains its maximum $d$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0F3K" data-tag="0F3K">37.76.6</a>. We will use induction on $d$. Consider the open subscheme $Y_ d = \{ y \in Y \mid (\int _ f w)(y) = d\} $ of $Y$ and recall that $f^{-1}(Y_ d) \to Y_ d$ is finite, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0F3L" data-tag="0F3L">37.76.7</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0F3R" data-tag="0F3R">37.77.1</a> for every affine open $W \subset Y$ we have that $Y_ d \cap W$ is affine (this uses that $W \times _ Y X$ is affine, being affine over $X$). Hence $Y_ d \to Y$ is an affine morphism of schemes. We conclude that $f^{-1}(Y_ d) = Y_ d \times _ Y X$ is an affine scheme being affine over $X$. Then $f^{-1}(Y_ d) \to Y_ d$ is surjective and hence $Y_ d$ is affine by Limits, Lemma <a href="/tag/01ZT" data-tag="01ZT">32.11.1</a>. If $Y_ d = Y$, then the affine stratification number of $Y$ is $0$ and we conclude the lemma is true. If not, then let $Y' = Y \setminus Y_ d$ viewed as a reduced closed subscheme of $Y$. Set $X' = Y' \times _ Y X$. Since $X'$ is closed in $X$ it is affine. Since $Y'$ is closed in $Y$ it is separated. The morphism $f' : X' \to Y'$ is surjective and $w$ induces a weighting $w'$ of $f'$ (see Lemma <a href="/tag/0F3B" data-tag="0F3B">37.75.3</a>) and $\int _{f'} w'$ is the restriction of $\int _ f w$ to $Y'$. By induction $Y'$ has an affine stratification of length $\leq $ the number of distinct values of $\int _{f'} w'$ minus $1$ and the proof is complete. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>